abstract: >-
  Lean 4 formalization, manuscript, audit document and reproduction material
  for f(7,7), the maximum number of boundary intersection points of two simple
  heptagons. This is the smallest open odd-odd case of the
  Dillencourt-Mount-Saalfeld problem.
  The kernel-checked theorem is Pair9Shift.points9_le_38_of_coincFree, with
  isGreatest_38_of_coincFree. Its hypotheses: each heptagon is simple as a
  point set (collinear vertices allowed), the boundaries meet in finitely many
  points, and no vertex of one equals a vertex of the other. Its conclusion:
  at most 38 boundary intersection points, with 38 attained by an explicit
  integer certificate. The theorem covers every pair in general position, and
  allows a vertex of one polygon to lie in the interior of a side of the other.
  The proof excludes 40 and 42 intersections by two forbidden local crossing
  patterns: one with 8 prescribed incidences, which has a human proof, and one
  with 14 prescribed incidences, refuted by a solver and replayed in Lean.
  A machine-checked covering theorem shows that every candidate crossing
  pattern contains one of the two. Further steps transfer the result from
  sign data to the real plane and remove vertex-side contacts.
  The unrestricted f(7,7) = 38 additionally uses the external vertex-coincidence
  reduction of Ackerman-Keszegh-Rote, Sect. 9.1. That reduction is not
  formalized, and its applicability to these definitions is unresolved.
  Substantial portions of the work were produced with generative-AI
  assistance; see the manuscript's disclosure.
keywords:
  - discrete geometry
  - computational geometry
  - simple polygons
  - polygon intersections
  - Dillencourt-Mount-Saalfeld conjecture
  - order types
  - chirotopes
  - computer-assisted proof
  - formal verification
  - Lean 4
